WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.700
Hello, and welcome to your second drink of Dream Team.

2
00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:10.220
We have some really great behind-the-scenes tidbits about this episode

3
00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.260
straight from Brian Baumgartner.

4
00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:13.060
Oh, yeah.

5
00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:17.280
I mean, that storyline with Kevin being at front reception is so good.

6
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.620
I was curious if the scene had any extra moments

7
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.480
that didn't make it in the original broadcaSt.

8
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:24.760
And I found this funny talking head.

9
00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:27.580
It's in the shooting draft that Kevin would have said.

10
00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.180
So I'm going to set it up, okay?

11
00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.020
Kevin is answering the phones, and it is not going well, right?

12
00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:37.040
Yeah, because Pam is off starting her new company with Michael.

13
00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:38.540
They need someone to sit at reception.

14
00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:39.240
That's right.

15
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.320
Pam has left, and Jim has this talking head.

16
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.860
You know, it made it into the episode where Jim says,

17
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.900
Charles is having Kevin cover the phones for a while.

18
00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.960
How do I say this diplomatically?

19
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.180
I think Kevin is doing exactly as well as anyone might have expected

20
00:00:55.180 --> 00:00:58.000
someone like him to perform in a position like that.

21
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:58.420
Mm-hmm.

22
00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.140
And then more phones ring and action happens.

23
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.840
But in the shooting draft, Kevin would have had a talking head

24
00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:06.440
immediately after that, and he would have said,

25
00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:08.060
I'm giving myself a B.

26
00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:09.720
A B.

27
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:11.380
He thinks he's doing okay.

28
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.800
Also in the shooting draft, Kevin would have answered the phone,

29
00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:17.860
and he would have said, Dunder Kevin, this is Kevin.

30
00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:19.080
Amazing.

31
00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:21.860
And I wonder, because what made it in the episode is

32
00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.480
Brian is Kevin saying, Dunder Mifflin, this is Kevin.

33
00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.500
And I wonder if Brian just said it properly.

34
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:31.680
Oh, he forgot to mess it up.

35
00:01:31.780 --> 00:01:32.200
Yes.

36
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:33.160
Maybe so.

37
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.940
Because the shooting draft said, Dunder Kevin, this is Kevin.

38
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:41.580
Well, lady, you and I saw a lot of background catches when we were breaking down this episode,

39
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:43.280
but I've got another one for you.

40
00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:47.540
This one comes in from Rennell in Brooklyn, New York, who says,

41
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.120
Ladies, I know you love a background catch.

42
00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:56.920
And at one minute and 56 seconds, when Pam is saying that today is her first day at her new job

43
00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.080
at Michael Scott Paper Company, and then she points to a sign in the lawn,

44
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:06.920
I thought at first Michael had put his own sign over a real estate sign,

45
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:10.380
but I think it's a sign for someone running for office.

46
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:11.740
Did you notice that?

47
00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.320
I did not catch that.

48
00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:14.400
I didn't catch that.

49
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.420
So I went to the script to see how it was described, and Rennell, you are correct.

50
00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:23.180
In the shooting draft, it says,

51
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:26.680
Exterior, Michael's condo morning, a sunny day.

52
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:28.980
Pam is dressed in a new skirt suit.

53
00:02:29.260 --> 00:02:32.320
She's posing like a spokesmodel for the camera's amusement

54
00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.880
by a sign stuck into Michael's front lawn that reads,

55
00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:38.200
Michael Scott Paper Company Incorporated.

56
00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:43.740
If we look closely, we can see four Congress peeking out under the new sign.

57
00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:52.720
So yes, they just slapped their new company name on someone else's sign.

58
00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:53.260
I know.

59
00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.200
It's like someone had to, like, running for Congress.

60
00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:55.840
Yes.

61
00:02:56.040 --> 00:02:57.200
I thought that was so great.

62
00:02:57.200 --> 00:02:58.400
I love that detail.

63
00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:03.280
And also, I love that they described Pam's outfit as a new skirt suit.

64
00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:03.820
Yes.

65
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:06.580
Well, you know, the network, it had to be a skirt.

66
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:07.300
I know.

67
00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.220
We talk about it.

68
00:03:08.320 --> 00:03:08.640
Mm-hmm.

69
00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:14.280
You know, I was actually curious if there were any candy bag alts for Pam's talking head

70
00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.200
about her starting her new job.

71
00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:16.680
Oh, yeah.

72
00:03:16.820 --> 00:03:17.220
There were.

73
00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:17.840
Many.

74
00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:18.480
Many?

75
00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:19.600
I can tell you there were.

76
00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:26.980
Well, I found two treasures that might give you all an idea of Pam's life at front reception.

77
00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:29.060
Jenna, you're probably going to remember these.

78
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:29.920
Here.

79
00:03:30.020 --> 00:03:31.260
Actually, why don't you read them?

80
00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:31.880
Oh, sure.

81
00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:32.360
Yes.

82
00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.180
Okay, here's one.

83
00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:36.540
This isn't crazy.

84
00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:38.760
People start companies all the time.

85
00:03:39.140 --> 00:03:43.940
And who better to start a paper company with than the best paper salesman Scranton has ever

86
00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:44.380
seen?

87
00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.460
Besides, I needed a change in scenery.

88
00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.000
Answering phones and faxing in Creed's prescriptions was wearing on me.

89
00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.120
What are Creed's prescriptions?

90
00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.840
I don't know, but I guess there were a lot.

91
00:03:56.080 --> 00:03:57.720
Okay, this next one really got me.

92
00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:58.300
Okay.

93
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:03.080
I have a ton of faith in Michael, but it's not just about that.

94
00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:04.760
I needed a change.

95
00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:09.720
There's only so many times you can debate with Kevin about pizza pockets versus pizza

96
00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:11.140
before you reach wit's end.

97
00:04:12.800 --> 00:04:15.700
I do feel like, you know, at reception, you got stuck.

98
00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:17.960
If someone came up there, right?

99
00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:23.220
And Kevin, in the early seasons, would go up to Pam and like lean on reception and talk.

100
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:23.960
Oh, my gosh.

101
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:24.780
Talk her ear off.

102
00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:28.760
Well, we had another fun background catch from Victoria S.

103
00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.880
from Langley, British Columbia,

104
00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:36.340
who said, at eight minutes and five seconds, I noticed that Pam or Michael doesn't lock

105
00:04:36.340 --> 00:04:38.720
the door when they leave Michael's condo.

106
00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.980
Why does this happen all the time in movies or was it intentional?

107
00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.380
Well, you know, I was re-watching this and they don't.

108
00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:46.860
You're right.

109
00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:47.700
They just leave.

110
00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:53.360
And I thought maybe, though, my front door locks automatically.

111
00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.280
I mean, you're right.

112
00:04:55.480 --> 00:04:56.140
That can happen.

113
00:04:56.140 --> 00:05:00.660
I actually changed the lock on my front door because we got locked out so many times.

114
00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:04.140
Oh, so you have to lock it with a key.

115
00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:05.080
We have to lock it.

116
00:05:05.300 --> 00:05:05.680
Okay.

117
00:05:06.120 --> 00:05:10.880
Yeah, but like just to say they do not deadbolt it or anything.

118
00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.880
And I think that's for sake of time in the episode.

119
00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:15.680
Like we did just have to get out the door.

120
00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.280
Like we didn't have the time to do the business of getting out our keys and locking it.

121
00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:21.720
The authenticity of leaving.

122
00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:22.480
Right, right.

123
00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.740
I mean, that happens a lot in movies and TV.

124
00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:26.960
Like you just hop out of a car.

125
00:05:27.100 --> 00:05:29.160
You don't even take off a seatbelt or something, you know, it's like.

126
00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.280
Well, it's also how no one says goodbye.

127
00:05:31.700 --> 00:05:33.160
They never finish a phone call.

128
00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:34.180
Oh, yeah.

129
00:05:34.340 --> 00:05:39.700
Like when you're done talking to someone on the phone in a movie, you just like go away.

130
00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:40.760
It's so funny.

131
00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.780
Like it takes me forever to get off of a phone call.

132
00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:44.900
I'm like, okay, well, anyway, I guess I'll talk to you soon.

133
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:45.960
And yeah, have a good time.

134
00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:46.280
All right.

135
00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:46.780
Okay, bye.

136
00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:47.280
Bye.

137
00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:47.360
Bye.

138
00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:48.300
Like that.

139
00:05:48.440 --> 00:05:50.460
You never get that in the movies either.

140
00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:52.260
I want to see that in movies.

141
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:52.980
Right.

142
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:54.120
I want someone to do that.

143
00:05:54.220 --> 00:05:54.620
I know.

144
00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.260
I did think about you though, Jenna, as you were talking about the business of locking

145
00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:04.980
the door because I feel like if you were in a play, you would do that because it would

146
00:06:04.980 --> 00:06:06.780
ground you to the realness.

147
00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:12.860
When I saw you in the play Ashland Avenue in Chicago and you were so good in it, but you

148
00:06:12.860 --> 00:06:17.200
had to go over and like get yourself a cup of coffee and you did all of it.

149
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:18.560
You actually did the business.

150
00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:24.000
Even there was like a sink that you would go and you actually did all the things.

151
00:06:24.380 --> 00:06:24.600
Yeah.

152
00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.160
I mean, I like doing all the business.

153
00:06:27.500 --> 00:06:34.980
I do find it really grounding and like maybe to the point where like I have a thing that

154
00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.900
I'm always pitching whenever I have a role and.

155
00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:38.680
What is it?

156
00:06:38.740 --> 00:06:39.660
Your face just lit up.

157
00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:40.800
Well, here's the thing.

158
00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:42.860
It really bugs me.

159
00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:48.240
Like, especially if you're playing a parent and you have to like walk up or down stairs

160
00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:54.060
or walk from room to room in a scene, I'm sorry, if you are a parent, you are constantly

161
00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:55.620
carrying something in your hand.

162
00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:56.180
It's true.

163
00:06:56.380 --> 00:06:56.540
Right?

164
00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:01.120
Like if you're at the bottom of the stairs, you collect stuff and you carry it up the stairs

165
00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:02.740
and then vice versa.

166
00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:03.080
Like.

167
00:07:03.580 --> 00:07:04.440
Cups and bowls.

168
00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:05.460
Cups and bowls.

169
00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:07.600
I'm always carrying them from downstairs.

170
00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:08.960
That would be my pitch.

171
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:15.660
Like I should not be walking empty handed down the stairs as I'm ready to go out the door.

172
00:07:15.660 --> 00:07:21.940
Like there's business, you need the business of life because also we're all trying to make

173
00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.400
as few trips as possible.

174
00:07:23.660 --> 00:07:24.800
So that's my other thing.

175
00:07:24.980 --> 00:07:30.820
Like if someone is carrying plates in from the table, don't give them two plates, give

176
00:07:30.820 --> 00:07:35.940
them a pile of plates and they're using the, they're using their knee to open the door.

177
00:07:36.340 --> 00:07:37.520
Like that's life.

178
00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:39.400
So I love that business.

179
00:07:39.400 --> 00:07:42.460
And of course, you know, I don't know.

180
00:07:42.460 --> 00:07:46.460
They're just like, please, I don't need your specificity.

181
00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.320
And I'm like, well, I think it would be welcome.

182
00:07:48.580 --> 00:07:53.820
I can't wait to go see you in a play and watch you carry a bunch of stuff down some

183
00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:54.240
stairs.

184
00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:54.680
Okay.

185
00:07:54.980 --> 00:07:57.460
We have one last letter and it's from Caitlin M.

186
00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:58.340
in Norfolk, Virginia.

187
00:07:58.820 --> 00:08:01.520
And I thought this was really sweet and I wanted to share it.

188
00:08:02.080 --> 00:08:03.420
Caitlin says, hi, office ladies.

189
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.180
One of my favorite moments from this episode is when Pam and Michael are talking in the

190
00:08:07.180 --> 00:08:08.920
driveway toward the end of the episode.

191
00:08:08.920 --> 00:08:12.520
This scene shows how Michael can be a great communicator.

192
00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:18.360
After Pam's management of their day, comforting Michael, setting realistic goals, et cetera,

193
00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:22.700
she has a vulnerable moment and out of frustration makes a hurtful comment.

194
00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:28.360
Michael takes a moment to process it, steals his nerves, and decides not to respond directly.

195
00:08:28.980 --> 00:08:34.040
Instead, he calmly explains their situation in simple terms, provides a relevant personal

196
00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:40.440
anecdote that inspires her confidence and explains which actions he will take to remedy the situation.

197
00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:46.060
This takes the onus off of Pam to make decisions and or take action while she's feeling acutely

198
00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:49.560
distressed, just like she did for Michael earlier that day.

199
00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.260
I love this scene so much.

200
00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.800
Michael was focused, firm, constructive, and empathetic.

201
00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:58.920
Scenes like these, where his emotional intelligence shines through, really balance him out as a character.

202
00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:01.000
Thank you for making this wonderful podcaSt.

203
00:09:01.000 --> 00:09:05.000
You're a bright spot in my busy days as a medical student in clerkships.

204
00:09:05.500 --> 00:09:07.240
Aw, thank you.

205
00:09:07.380 --> 00:09:11.660
I love hearing how that scene landed on Caitlin.

206
00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:12.440
I know.

207
00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:18.400
And just what the show means to people throughout their day, you know, and how they hear it and

208
00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:19.700
listen to it and process it.

209
00:09:20.700 --> 00:09:23.160
Well, thank you, everybody, for writing in.

210
00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.020
You can write to us in our second drinks folder over on officeladies.com.

