254
00:11:11.190 --> 00:11:12.970
2022, they're coming at you.

255
00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:14.330
It's all happening, guys.

256
00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.710
Angela Martin Fashion is here, mixed in with a little Jenna Fischer.

257
00:11:17.710 --> 00:11:22.290
A lot of people wrote in to say the fakie flower was from Sex and the City.

258
00:11:22.530 --> 00:11:24.710
I guess Carrie Bradshaw loved a fakie flower.

259
00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:27.310
She is not the OG of the fakie flower.

260
00:11:27.450 --> 00:11:28.010
Who is?

261
00:11:28.010 --> 00:11:30.290
I mean, I can't even tell you how much mail we got about it.

262
00:11:30.410 --> 00:11:32.750
I mean, I feel like fakie flower was here in the 80s.

263
00:11:32.870 --> 00:11:34.390
Let's look up some Molly Ringwald.

264
00:11:34.630 --> 00:11:35.090
Come on.

265
00:11:35.370 --> 00:11:36.210
I'm with you there.

266
00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:36.550
Yeah.

267
00:11:36.990 --> 00:11:42.330
I'm positive I had a fakie flower I clipped on a hat because I was trying to be like,

268
00:11:42.370 --> 00:11:43.690
what was that movie, Pretty in Pink?

269
00:11:43.690 --> 00:11:48.830
I have decided that I'm going to single-handedly bring back the fakie flower during our book tour.

270
00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:53.990
Even though Drew Barrymore wore one on her show, I'm going to bring it wide in 2022.

271
00:11:54.650 --> 00:11:55.610
Bring it wide?

272
00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:56.730
Yeah, I am.

273
00:11:56.850 --> 00:11:58.710
That should be our book tour slogan.

274
00:11:58.890 --> 00:11:59.550
Bring it wide.

275
00:11:59.750 --> 00:11:59.950
No.

276
00:12:00.230 --> 00:12:00.870
You know what?

277
00:12:00.970 --> 00:12:01.250
I don't.

278
00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:01.970
Let's rethink that.

279
00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:02.570
We should not.

280
00:12:02.770 --> 00:12:03.170
You know what?

281
00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:03.950
I'm going to think on it.

282
00:12:04.190 --> 00:12:04.490
Okay.

283
00:12:04.730 --> 00:12:05.450
Bring your flower.

284
00:12:05.630 --> 00:12:05.850
No.

285
00:12:06.510 --> 00:12:06.790
Wait.

286
00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:07.850
Maybe?

287
00:12:08.550 --> 00:12:08.950
No.

288
00:12:10.150 --> 00:12:11.350
We'll see you on the book tour.

289
00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:19.430
But for today, we're going to be discussing Dream Team, which is Season 5, Episode 20 of

290
00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.430
The Office, written by B.J.

291
00:12:21.510 --> 00:12:23.370
Novak and directed by Paul Feig.

292
00:12:23.510 --> 00:12:24.610
Let's have a summary.

293
00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:25.790
Here it is.

294
00:12:26.170 --> 00:12:31.570
Michael has officially left Dunder Mifflin, and he has started his own paper company called

295
00:12:31.570 --> 00:12:34.810
Michael Scott Paper Company in his condo.

296
00:12:35.270 --> 00:12:36.470
Pam has joined him.

297
00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:42.730
Michael is having a little trouble facing his workday, but with Pam's encouragement, he decides

298
00:12:42.730 --> 00:12:48.350
to assemble a dream team to get their company started, and they are also going to have a

299
00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:49.950
meeting with a potential investor.

300
00:12:50.390 --> 00:12:51.490
Things are looking up.

301
00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:51.950
Yeah.

302
00:12:52.490 --> 00:12:56.090
Meanwhile, back at the office, Charles is in charge.

303
00:12:56.370 --> 00:12:57.230
Charles in charge.

304
00:12:57.350 --> 00:12:57.690
I know.

305
00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:58.570
In Scranton.

306
00:12:59.210 --> 00:12:59.310
Hmm.

307
00:13:00.930 --> 00:13:05.770
Andy discovers that Charles is a soccer fan, and now everybody is trying to kiss up to Charles

308
00:13:05.770 --> 00:13:06.850
and get on his good side.

309
00:13:07.010 --> 00:13:09.250
It's the first time we've seen the man smile.

310
00:13:10.070 --> 00:13:13.370
Jim is even going to pretend that he used to play soccer.

311
00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:14.970
This does not go well.

312
00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:15.590
No.

313
00:13:15.970 --> 00:13:18.510
You know what I loved about this storyline, and it's something B.J.

314
00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:19.190
talked about in the

315
00:13:19.190 --> 00:13:20.730
DVD commentary for this episode?

316
00:13:20.890 --> 00:13:24.710
He said normally when you break a story, you have the A story, which is the main story,

317
00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:28.610
and then you have the B story, which is sort of like the other sort of side dish, if you will.

318
00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:34.530
He said that Idris's scenes were so great, and the dynamic that Charles Minor had with

319
00:13:34.530 --> 00:13:36.710
the rest of the office was so interesting for them.

320
00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:40.390
They really felt like his storyline as two A stories.

321
00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.070
It felt like that.

322
00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:42.470
Yeah.

323
00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:43.270
Yeah.

324
00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.690
This felt like a very well-balanced episode.

325
00:13:47.030 --> 00:13:47.370
Right.

326
00:13:47.550 --> 00:13:48.490
I thought that was interesting.

327
00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:54.810
Fast fact number one, this episode starts a delightful arc where Michael is going to run

328
00:13:54.810 --> 00:13:55.970
his own paper company.

329
00:13:55.970 --> 00:14:01.410
And I found out that it was our writer, Charlie Grandy, who came up with this idea, and they

330
00:14:01.410 --> 00:14:03.610
had been planning this arc for months.

331
00:14:03.870 --> 00:14:04.070
Oh.

332
00:14:04.250 --> 00:14:06.970
Well, he was on the DVD commentary for this episode.

333
00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:11.550
I don't know if they mentioned this on the commentary, but this episode was nominated for

334
00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:16.170
an Emmy for Outstanding Single Camera Editing for a comedy series.

335
00:14:16.170 --> 00:14:17.710
Our editors were nominated.

336
00:14:17.830 --> 00:14:18.430
That's fantastic.

337
00:14:18.850 --> 00:14:20.930
It was edited by Claire Scanlon.

338
00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:27.530
Now, Claire came in to replace Dean Holland when he moved over to work as an editor and

339
00:14:27.530 --> 00:14:29.870
eventually a director on Parks and Recreation.

340
00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:34.170
So now our two editors were Dave Rogers and Claire Scanlon.

341
00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:40.290
You might remember Claire because she had a cameo as Freckles in Lecture Circuit Part 2.

342
00:14:41.070 --> 00:14:47.410
Before this, she edited Golden Ticket, but Dream Team was her second episode, and she was nominated

343
00:14:47.410 --> 00:14:48.290
for an Emmy.

344
00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:48.950
Claire.

345
00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:49.610
Claire.

346
00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:54.390
Of course now, Claire is like big, badass director, amazing.

347
00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:56.210
I would love to have Claire on.

348
00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:57.710
We should have Claire on.

349
00:14:57.950 --> 00:14:58.000
I know.

350
00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.950
Fast fact number two is a location breakdown.

351
00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:03.490
I knew you'd have one.

352
00:15:03.490 --> 00:15:09.970
Well, a good deal of this episode takes place at Michael's Condo, and this was not an easy

353
00:15:09.970 --> 00:15:10.430
feat.

354
00:15:11.190 --> 00:15:15.930
Randy Cordray told me we went back to the same condo complex where we shot Michael's

355
00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:20.270
Condo before for dinner party and pretty much any episode featuring Michael's Condo.

356
00:15:20.490 --> 00:15:26.770
It was in Reseda, California, and it was owned now by a married couple.

357
00:15:26.770 --> 00:15:32.830
In order to use their condo, we had to move them into a hotel for an entire week.

358
00:15:33.590 --> 00:15:36.050
We had to take all of their stuff out.

359
00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:38.350
They put it into cartons in the garage.

360
00:15:38.710 --> 00:15:42.350
We painted the entire downstairs and decorated it.

361
00:15:42.590 --> 00:15:48.330
They made a point to decorate it differently from dinner party because, you know, now Michael

362
00:15:48.330 --> 00:15:48.890
is single.

363
00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:53.890
Yes, I noticed the only thing that remained that I recognized from dinner party was the

364
00:15:53.890 --> 00:15:55.410
St. Pauly girl beer sign.

365
00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:56.190
Same.

366
00:15:56.190 --> 00:15:57.430
In the dining room.

367
00:15:57.650 --> 00:15:57.870
Yes.

368
00:15:58.090 --> 00:16:03.930
We only shot at this condo for one day, and then they had to completely restore it back

369
00:16:03.930 --> 00:16:05.190
to the way the couple had it.

370
00:16:05.470 --> 00:16:10.110
And here is the thing that is fascinating to me, because what they would do is they would

371
00:16:10.110 --> 00:16:11.970
take like a gazillion photos.

372
00:16:12.230 --> 00:16:12.390
Yeah.

373
00:16:12.690 --> 00:16:15.470
Of every shelf, of everything.

374
00:16:16.390 --> 00:16:19.810
Anything that we moved, they took a picture of it before they moved it, and they would put

375
00:16:19.810 --> 00:16:24.810
it in a binder, like hard copy photos, because back then, you know, that's what it was.

376
00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:26.510
Yeah, so it would be like kitchen counter.

377
00:16:26.910 --> 00:16:27.310
Yes.

378
00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:27.610
Yeah.

379
00:16:27.690 --> 00:16:32.710
So that when the people came back, like even their little spoon caddy was where they left

380
00:16:32.710 --> 00:16:32.970
it.

381
00:16:32.990 --> 00:16:35.210
Or if they had a bookshelf, all their stuff.

382
00:16:35.370 --> 00:16:37.030
The books were in the same order as before.

383
00:16:37.030 --> 00:16:38.850
The picture frames were right where they left them.

384
00:16:38.970 --> 00:16:39.350
Everything.

385
00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:39.790
Everything.

386
00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:41.470
Isn't that fascinating?

387
00:16:41.770 --> 00:16:42.050
I know.

388
00:16:42.250 --> 00:16:42.890
It is amazing.

389
00:16:43.190 --> 00:16:48.550
In the DVD commentary, BJ talked about how carefully they decorated every wall.

390
00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:52.950
They put so much thought and detail, and you just see it just quickly in the background.

391
00:16:53.230 --> 00:16:54.370
But I caught a few things.

392
00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:55.410
I'm sure you guys did, too.

393
00:16:55.470 --> 00:16:56.870
I'm going to bring them up when we get to it.

394
00:16:57.130 --> 00:16:57.810
I love it.

395
00:16:57.810 --> 00:17:06.590
Fast fact number three is a guest star homage to the amazing actress who played Nana.

396
00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:09.110
Her name is Connie Sawyer.

397
00:17:09.510 --> 00:17:10.670
Just incredible.

398
00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:14.690
We got a piece of mail from Meredith Q, who wrote,

399
00:17:14.930 --> 00:17:16.310
I did a deep dive.

400
00:17:16.530 --> 00:17:19.490
The woman who played Nana needs some recognition.

401
00:17:19.910 --> 00:17:21.010
Yes, she does.

402
00:17:21.370 --> 00:17:23.450
First of all, she lived to be 105.

403
00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:25.150
Who does that?

404
00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:25.590
Wow.

405
00:17:25.590 --> 00:17:26.650
Is what Meredith has said.

406
00:17:27.070 --> 00:17:32.810
And according to IMDb, her first acting credit was in 1953 at the age of 40.

407
00:17:33.050 --> 00:17:37.910
Her last acting credit was in 2014 at the age of 101.

408
00:17:38.930 --> 00:17:43.350
That's 61 years of acting, plus she acted over the age of 100.

409
00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:44.670
What a rock star.

410
00:17:44.910 --> 00:17:45.790
Tell me everything.

411
00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:52.630
Well, I looked into this woman, and performance had been a part of her life her whole life.

412
00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:54.430
She had done vaudeville.

413
00:17:54.430 --> 00:17:56.170
She had performed live with Frank Sinatra.

414
00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:01.890
But it's true that she never had a film or television role until she was 40.

415
00:18:02.410 --> 00:18:05.670
So on the commentary, the writers were talking about her on set.

416
00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.070
They said she had the most amazing stories.

417
00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:11.590
She was throwing out stories about Bing Crosby, Bob Hope.

418
00:18:11.750 --> 00:18:13.170
They were just like, oh my gosh.

419
00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:14.170
She was fascinating.

420
00:18:14.590 --> 00:18:18.750
What I love about her story, Jenna, is that she did stage shows for years.

421
00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.350
She didn't get an on-camera role till 40.

422
00:18:21.510 --> 00:18:22.670
But this was her passion.

423
00:18:22.870 --> 00:18:24.970
And you should never let anyone take that away from you.

424
00:18:25.090 --> 00:18:29.110
However your passion manifests itself, I just love her story.

425
00:18:29.110 --> 00:18:30.510
She just kept at it.

426
00:18:30.650 --> 00:18:36.190
She has over 140 acting credits, and almost all of them are small roles.

427
00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:39.930
And she has this quote about her acting career where she said,

428
00:18:40.270 --> 00:18:41.450
Life is fun.

429
00:18:41.730 --> 00:18:45.050
That has nothing to do with whether you become a star or not.

430
00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:48.750
And if the parts aren't too large, I can go around talking to everybody.

431
00:18:48.950 --> 00:18:50.550
It's like going to a party.

432
00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:51.490
Aww.

433
00:18:52.110 --> 00:18:59.690
And when she was 104, she wrote an autobiography called, I Never Wanted to Be a Star, and I Wasn't.

434
00:18:59.990 --> 00:19:02.850
And she self-published it in 2017.

435
00:19:03.590 --> 00:19:04.550
At 104!

436
00:19:05.150 --> 00:19:05.630
Wow.

437
00:19:06.210 --> 00:19:07.750
That is amazing.

438
00:19:08.190 --> 00:19:14.070
BJ said the scenes that you guys did with her, that when they threw out some improv, she didn't miss a beat.

439
00:19:14.350 --> 00:19:16.530
Well, I remember her from when Harry met Sally.

440
00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:22.770
She is one of the women in the couples that they interview documentary style.

441
00:19:23.210 --> 00:19:24.630
She has no lines.

442
00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:29.650
Her husband speaks the whole time, but it all plays out on her face.

443
00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.110
And she is so good.

444
00:19:31.250 --> 00:19:36.370
And she has this story where she says that she read the script, and she went to Rob Reiner, and she said,

445
00:19:36.450 --> 00:19:41.610
I want to be the woman in the deli who says the famous line, I'll have what she's having.

446
00:19:41.950 --> 00:19:44.170
She said, I want the show-stopping line.

447
00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:46.890
And he said, I'm sorry, we already shot that.

448
00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:47.750
My mother did it.

449
00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:48.990
Yeah, his mom did it.

450
00:19:49.830 --> 00:19:51.690
But she's just so good.

451
00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:55.050
She passed away in 2018 at the age of 105.

452
00:19:55.590 --> 00:20:01.950
And at the time of her death, she was the oldest working actress in Hollywood and the oldest member of the Screen Actors Guild Union.

453
00:20:02.490 --> 00:20:03.270
Amazing woman.

454
00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:04.050
Amazing.

455
00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:05.750
Connie Sawyer, everybody.

456
00:20:06.310 --> 00:20:08.270
Well, that's all I've got for Fast Facts.

457
00:20:08.270 --> 00:20:14.350
But before we go to break, you know, Marcus York, who played Billy Merchant, is in this episode.

458
00:20:14.650 --> 00:20:19.570
This is his last appearance on the show, and he actually passed away earlier this year.

459
00:20:19.950 --> 00:20:23.210
He was just wonderful, and he was so wonderful to us.

460
00:20:23.210 --> 00:20:24.510
He shared a story with us.

461
00:20:24.650 --> 00:20:25.130
He did.

462
00:20:25.330 --> 00:20:28.170
He and I would text, and we talked on the phone several times.

463
00:20:28.410 --> 00:20:31.150
He loved his character of Billy Merchant.

464
00:20:31.330 --> 00:20:35.730
He was so supportive of the show, and we were just so fond of him.

465
00:20:35.730 --> 00:20:40.810
We got a letter from a man named Nathan N., and I thought this summed up Marcus pretty well.

466
00:20:41.170 --> 00:20:42.050
He said,

467
00:20:42.410 --> 00:20:49.770
I just wanted to comment on Marcus York, because I believe this is his last episode, and I wanted to let people know what kind of guy he is.

468
00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:54.670
He was from Dark County, Ohio, which is near where I work as a police officer.

469
00:20:55.370 --> 00:21:01.630
Just so happens that my partner met him one night when he was checking on him when he pulled over to use his phone.

470
00:21:02.610 --> 00:21:09.590
Upon Marcus telling him he was on the office, my partner said that he's never watched the show, but that I did,

471
00:21:09.590 --> 00:21:13.950
and I was going to be so bummed that I missed him, because I've talked about him in the paSt.

472
00:21:14.910 --> 00:21:19.510
Marcus asked him if I was working, and he said I wouldn't be in for about an hour.

473
00:21:19.890 --> 00:21:25.790
Not only did Marcus wait that hour, but on top of that, he took a picture with me, his idea,

474
00:21:25.790 --> 00:21:30.570
hung out for an hour, and gave me an autographed picture made out to my wife.

475
00:21:30.810 --> 00:21:35.510
He seemed so happy to meet a fan that knew him and was interested in him and his career.

476
00:21:35.910 --> 00:21:40.670
And even though I only met him that one time, he definitely left a lasting impression for sure.

477
00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:43.270
Such a nice guy and a tragic loss.

478
00:21:43.470 --> 00:21:44.630
That is so well said.

479
00:21:44.630 --> 00:21:46.410
He was such a nice person.

480
00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:55.830
And he was so aware of the joy that he could bring to people by just being open to that fan experience or talking about his time on the show.

481
00:21:56.070 --> 00:21:58.430
And he certainly did that with us here at Office Ladies.

482
00:21:58.590 --> 00:21:58.970
He did.

483
00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:01.070
Much love to Marcus and his family.

484
00:22:01.490 --> 00:22:02.790
All right, let's go to break.

485
00:22:03.010 --> 00:22:03.770
Let's do it.

486
00:22:13.100 --> 00:22:20.840
Well, we are back from break, and before we get into Dream Team, I couldn't help but remember the OG Dream Team, Jenna.

487
00:22:21.340 --> 00:22:22.340
Was it basketball?

488
00:22:22.980 --> 00:22:23.460
Yes!

489
00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:30.560
Yes, you guys, the 1992 United States Men's Olympic Basketball Team, nicknamed the Dream Team.

490
00:22:30.820 --> 00:22:37.000
It was the first American Olympic team to feature active professional players from the National Basketball Association.

491
00:22:37.560 --> 00:22:43.520
And the team has been described from journalists around the world as the greatest sports team ever assembled.

492
00:22:43.840 --> 00:22:44.360
Wow.

493
00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:45.320
I know!

494
00:22:45.540 --> 00:22:47.120
I mean, I totally remember that.

495
00:22:47.340 --> 00:22:47.900
Me too!

496
00:22:48.040 --> 00:22:49.040
It was a big deal.

497
00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:50.380
It was a big deal, you guys.

498
00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:52.240
And listen, this is like when I was in school.

499
00:22:52.240 --> 00:22:57.620
So, when this episode was coming up, whenever I saw the title Dream Team, I just always think...

500
00:22:57.620 --> 00:22:58.920
That's what I think of, too!

501
00:22:59.200 --> 00:22:59.460
Yeah!

502
00:22:59.660 --> 00:23:04.520
I don't know if you guys think of that, but I just want to read the roster and just a few stats, okay, of this team.

